","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σn_products * S_products - Σn_reactants * S_subunds

where n_products and n_reactants are the number of moles of products and reactants, respectively, and S_products and S_reactants are their standard molar entropies.

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + 3 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

The standard molar entropies (J/mol·K) are:

S_CO(g) = 188.6
S_O2(g) = 205.1
S_CO2(g) = 213.6

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = (3 mol * 213.6 J/mol·K) - (3 mol * 188.6 J/mol·K) - (3 mol * 205.1 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 681.8 J/K - 565.8 J/K - 615.1 J/K

ΔS = -509.1 J/K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -509.1 J/K.
