Calculate the lattice energy of CaCl2 given that the distance between the Ca2+ and Cl- ions in the crystal lattice is 0.231 nm and the Born-Lande equation constant is 2.21 x 10^-19 J nm.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The Born-Lande equation is given by:  
\[
U = \frac{2 \cdot e^2}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r} \cdot \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right)
\]  
where:  
- \( U \) is the lattice energy,  
- \( e \) is the electron charge (\( 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \)),  
- \( \varepsilon_0 \) is the vacuum permittivity (\( 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{C}^2/\text{N} \cdot \text{m}^2 \)),  
- \( r \) is the distance between the ions (\( 0.231 \, \text{nm} = 0.231 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \)),  
- \( n \) is the Madelung constant, which depends on the crystal lattice structure. For CaCl2, \( n = 1.72 \).  
- \( 2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J nm} \) is the Born-Lande equation constant, but it is not used in the standard Born-Lande equation.  

Substituting the values:  
\[
U = \frac{2 \times (1.602 \times 10^{-19})^2}{4 \pi \times 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \times 0.231 \times 10^{-9}} \times (1 - \frac{1}{1.72})
\]  
\[
U = -1.22 \times 10^{-18} \, \text{J}
\]  

The lattice energy is \(\boxed{-1.22 \times 10^{-18} \, \text{J}}\).
