"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This results in a symmetrical distribution of electron density.  
- **π molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated in two pairs of p-orbitals, above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This results in a non-symmetrical distribution of electron density, resembling a pair of lobes.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals along the axis, while π orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals perpendicular to the axis.  

$\boxed{\text{σ: electron density along the axis, symmetrical}} \\ \boxed{\text{π: electron density in lobes above/below the axis, non-symmetrical}}$
