","To prepare a selectively deuterium-labeled alkane from an alkene, you can use **deymmetric deuteration** using a reagent like **D₂/Pd-C** (deuterium gas with palladium-carbon as a catalyst). This method selectively adds deuterium to the less substituted carbon of the double bond, leading to a selective deuterium-labeled alkane. The reaction proceeds via anti-Markovnikov addition, where the deuterium adds to the less substituted carbon, and the proton (H) adds to the more substituted carbon.

The reaction can be summarized as:
\[
\text{R-CH=CH-R'} + \text{D}_2 \xrightarrow{\text{Pd-C}} \text{R-CH}_2\text{CDH-R'}
\]
where R and R' are alkyl or aryl groups.

The selectivity arises because the deuterium adds to the less substituted carbon due to the steric and electronic factors. The steric hindrance around the more substituted carbon makes it less accessible for the deuterium, while the electron-rich nature of the less substituted carbon makes it more susceptible to the deuterium.

For example, if you have an unsymmetrical alkene like 2-methyl-1-butene, the deuteration with D₂/Pd-C will yield 2-methyl-1-deututeropbutane as the major product.

\boxed{\text{CH}_3-\text{CH}=\text{CH}-\text{CH}_3 \xrightarrow{\text{D}_2/\text{Pd-C}} \text{CH}_3-\text{CH}_2\text{CDH}-\text{CH}_3}
