","The electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles in a solution of known ionic strength is significantly affected by the pH of the solution. The relationship between pH and electrokinetic potential is primarily due to the ionization of surface functional groups on the colloidal particles. For example, if the particles have acidic functional groups (e.g., carboxyl or carboxylate groups), the particles will have a higher negative charge at higher pH values (due to ionization of the acidic groups). Conversely, if the particles have basic functional groups (e.g., amino groups), the particles will have a higher positive charge at higher pH values (due to ionization of the basic groups).

The electrokinetic potential is measured as the electrophoretic mobility of the particles, which is the velocity of the particles in response to an electric field. The electrokinetic potential is directly proportional to the electrophoretic mobility. Therefore, the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles in a solution of known ionic strength will increase with increasing pH for acidic particles and decrease with increasing pH for basic particles.

In summary, the effect of pH on the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles in a solution of known ionic strength is due to the ionization of surface functional groups on the particles. The pH affects the charge of the particles, which in turn affects their electrophoretic mobility and the electrokinetic potential.
