","1. **Convert the speed of the train to meters per second**:  
   \( 45 \, \text{km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \, \text{m}}{1 \, \text{km}} \times \frac{1 \, \text{hr}}{60 \, \text{hr}} = 12 \, \text{m/s} \).  

2. **Determine the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger**:  
   Since the train and jogger move in the same direction, subtract the jogger's speed from the train's speed:  
   \( 12 \, \text{m/s} - 7 \, \text{m/s} = 5 \, \text{m/s} \).  

3. **Calculate the total distance the train must to to pass the jogger**:  
   The jogger is 150 m away from the train, and the entire train must cover this distance to pass the jogger.  

4. **Use the time and relative speed to find the length of the train**:  
   The time \( t = 25 \, \text{s} \), and the relative speed \( v = 5 \, \text{m/s} \).  
   The distance \( d = v \times t = 5 \, \text{m/s} \times 25 \, \text{s} = 125 \, \text{m} \).  

   The length of the train is \(\boxed{125 \, \text{m}}\).
