","To find the Fourier transform of the given function, we use the formula for the Fourier transform of a function \( f(t) \):

\[
F(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

For the given function \( f(t) = 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \), the Fourier transform is:

\[
F(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left( 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \right) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

This integral can be split into two separate integrals:

\[
F(\omega) = 3 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt + 2 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

These integrals can be evaluated using standard integration techniques (e.g., integration by parts). The final result is:

\[
F(\omega) = \frac{6\pi}{(\omega^2 + (2\pi)^2)} - \frac{4\pi}{(\omega^2 + (4\pi)^2)}
\]

This is the Fourier transform of the given function. It represents the amplitude of each frequency component in the function \( f(t) \).
