\[
\begin{pmatrix} m_t & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & m_t & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & m_t \end{pmatrix} \omega^2 \xi + \begin{pmatrix} k_t + K_c & -K_c & -K_c \\ -K_c & k_t + K_c & -K_c \\ -K_c & -K_c & k_t + K_c \end{pmatrix} \xi = 0.
\]  

To find the natural frequencies and mode shapes, we solve the eigenvalue problem:  
\[
(K - \omega^2 M) \xi = 0.
\]  
This leads to a characteristic equation that gives the natural frequencies \( \omega_i \). The corresponding eigenvalues \( \xi_i \) are the mode shapes.  

To examine the orthogonality of the mode shapes, we compute the dot product of the mode shapes:  
\[
\xi_i \cdot \xi_j = \sum_{i=1}^N \xi_{ij} \xi_{ij} = 0 \quad \text{for} \quad i \neq j.
\]  
If the dot product is zero for all \( i \neq j \), the mode shapes are orthogonal.  

The exact values of the natural frequencies and mode shapes can be found by solving the eigenvalue problem numerically.
