<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. The acceleration of the proton is then given by \(a = \frac{F}{m}\), where \(m\) is the mass of the proton. Substituting the given values:  
\[
a = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.60 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.0 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.0 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed is zero. The time \(t\) it takes the proton to reach a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by \(v = at\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.0 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 0.0825 \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time is \(\boxed{0.0825 \, \text{s}}\).

