<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The total momentum of the system (cannonball + Earth) before the collision is zero because both are at rest. After the collision, the momentum of the cannonball is \(mv = 1 \times 90 = 90 \, \text{kg m/s}\). To conserve momentum, the recoil velocity \(v_e\) of the Earth must be such that:

\[
v_e \times M = -90 \, \text{kg m/s}
\]

Solving for \(v_e\):

\[
v_e = \frac{-90}{M} = \frac{-90}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} = -1.5 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{m/s}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the opposite direction to the cannonball. The recoil velocity is extremely small, as expected due to the large mass of the Earth.

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

