A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To estimate the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength, we use the Hall-Oensley equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{G b}{d} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
where:  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(d\) is the diameter of the hard particles.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \, \text{N m}^{-2} \times 0.286 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m}}{10^{-8} \, \text{m}} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{1/2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 26 \times 0.286 \times 10^{-1} \times \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 1.05 \times 10^3 \, \text{N m}^{-2} = 1.05 \, \text{MPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{1.05 \, \text{MPa}}\).
