\[
\dot{H}(t) = -2w^2 H(t)
\]  
where:  
- \(\dot{H}(t)\) is the time derivative of the Hamiltonian \(H(t)\).  
- \(H(t)\) is the Hamiltonian of the harmonic oscillator.  
- \(w\) is the angular frequency of the oscillator.  

This equation shows that the time derivative of the Hamiltonian is proportional to the Hamiltonian itself and the negative of the square of the angular frequency.  

This relationship arises from the fact that the Hamiltonian of a harmonic oscillator is proportional to the square of the displacement and the square of the velocity. The time derivative of the Hamiltonian is then proportional to the product of the displacement and velocity, which in turn is related to the angular frequency.  

Thus, the Hamiltonian of a harmonic oscillator oscillates with a frequency of \(2w\).  

\[
\boxed{\dot{H}(t) = -2w^2 H(t)}
\]
