In the case of ocean currents, the Coriolis effect causes the currents to move in a spiral pattern, with the direction of the spiral depending on the direction of the original motion and the direction of the Earth's rotation. In general, the Corisoriolis effect causes currents moving in the same direction of the Earth's rotation to move to the right in the right hand of the Earth and to the left in the left hand of the Earth.

For example, the Gulf Stream flows from the hot skin of the sun to the top of the ocean, and the Coriolis effect causes it to move toward the right, causing it to flow up the way of the sun. The Kidoshio current flows from the hot skin of the sun to the top of the ocean, and the Coriolis effect causes it to move toward the right, causing it to flow up the way of the sun.

To calculate the magnitude of the Coriolis effect on ocean currents, we can use the following equation:

F_Coriolis = -2v * w

where:
- F_Coriolis is the Coriolis force,
- v is the velocity of the current,
- w is the angular velocity of the Earth (approximately 2.11 x 10^-4 rad/s).

The negative sign indicates that the Coriolis force is opposite to the direction of the relative velocity of the current.

