","The unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix is a fundamental property in quantum mechanics, ensuring the conservation of probability. The condition \(\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}\) ensures that the probability of the system transitioning from state \(m\) to state \(n\) is conserved. For a quantum mechanical system, the probability of the system transitioning from state \(m\) to state \(n\) is given by the square of the absolute square of the \(S_{lm}\) matrix element:  
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
S_{lm}^* S_{ln}
\end{pmatrix}
\end{pmatrix}
= \left| S_{lm} \right|^2
\]  
The sum over all possible intermediate states \(l\) gives the total probability of the system transitioning from state \(m\) to state \(n\):  
\[
\sum_l \left| S_{lm} \right|^2 = \sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln}
\]  
The unitarity condition ensures that this sum equals 1, i.e., the total probability is 1. The identity \(\delta_{mn}\) ensures that the probability is only 1 for the transition from state \(m\) to state \(n\), and 0 for any other transition.  

Thus, the unitarity condition ensures the conservation of probability in quantum mechanics.
